00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.550
The Post Office Made America.

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:17.449
It’s an ambitious claim, I know, but without
it the United States as we know it today would

00:00:17.449 --> 00:00:18.830
not exist.

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:23.490
As author Winifred Gallagher eloquently pointed
out in her book, “How the Post Office Created

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:27.280
America,” in the years following the American
Revolution, one didn’t say, “the United

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:30.779
States is,” they said, “the United States
are.”

00:00:30.779 --> 00:00:34.530
The country at the time was just a lose collectivity
of states.

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:38.780
The national government was weak—people
identified with their state first and their

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:39.940
country second.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:44.510
With the opening of the post office, however,
Americans across the continent had for the

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:49.140
first time a direct link to the federal government
and the outside world.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:53.280
In the New England colonies that preceded
the United States, infrastructure was poor.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.440
It was considered a great achievement when
travelers could finally reach New York from

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:59.000
Boston in under a week.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.680
There was little emphasis on creating roads
between colonies since each was a unique,

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:05.199
independent entity.

00:01:05.199 --> 00:01:09.149
Some have even attributed the colonist’s
lack of attachment to the crown to their lack

00:01:09.149 --> 00:01:10.149
of roads.

00:01:10.149 --> 00:01:14.880
Those living outside of cities might have
never met a royal official in their lives

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.759
just because of how hard it was to get around.

00:01:17.759 --> 00:01:22.790
Given this, the crown’s postal service in
the colonies was slow, expensive, and emphasized

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:27.770
links across the Atlantic to Britain almost
more than it did links between the colonies.

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:32.079
Before the American Revolution there were
unofficial, almost black market ways of getting

00:01:32.079 --> 00:01:36.840
mail around, but the increasingly legitimate
continental congress—the group that eventually

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:42.040
declared American independence—realized
that in order to properly coordinate and collaborate,

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:45.789
the member colonies needed a way to effectively
communicate.

00:01:45.789 --> 00:01:51.279
Therefore, surprisingly, the United States
Post Office was founded almost a full year

00:01:51.279 --> 00:01:53.960
before the United States itself.

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:58.869
In the century following its founding, the
US had a bit of a good problem—it expanded

00:01:58.869 --> 00:02:02.459
in territory faster than it could expand in
people.

00:02:02.459 --> 00:02:06.689
For much of its history and still to an extent
today, the United States was a country of

00:02:06.689 --> 00:02:09.129
vast, untamed wilderness.

00:02:09.129 --> 00:02:13.140
This was especially true in the early years
to the west of the Appalachians.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:18.760
Despite the gold rush, California only hit
a population of 1 million in about 1890.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:22.170
By that time the nation had a population of
over 60 million.

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:27.230
And between California and the Appalachians
during the early 1800s there was hardly anything—

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:28.730
especially roads.

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:31.530
California might as well have been an island.

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:35.890
To get there, most people and mail would take
a steamship down to Panama, cross the country

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:40.909
on land, and then reboard a ship to California
rather than attempting to cross the continent

00:02:40.909 --> 00:02:42.220
by land.

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:45.049
People were naturally afraid to settle the
frontier.

00:02:45.049 --> 00:02:50.469
The idea of being physically and informationally
detached from the civilized world while surrounded

00:02:50.469 --> 00:02:56.130
by untamed and dangerous wilderness made it
difficult for the new nation to push west.

00:02:56.130 --> 00:03:01.700
You have to remember that until the telegraph
became widespread in the 1850s and 60s, mail

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:06.150
was quite literally the only way to communicate
long-distance.

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:10.640
If you had a post office, you were connected,
if you didn’t, you were alone.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:15.569
Therefore, one of the first steps for a new
town to legitimize itself was to appeal to

00:03:15.569 --> 00:03:19.720
the federal government for a post office,
and this was usually granted.

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:25.189
Having a physical vestige of the federal government
in towns across the continent was immensely

00:03:25.189 --> 00:03:26.379
beneficial.

00:03:26.379 --> 00:03:30.989
Many would even say it played a large role
in America’s success.

00:03:30.989 --> 00:03:37.099
You see the US today as a fiercely nationalistic
country, but, as mentioned, for a while state

00:03:37.099 --> 00:03:42.739
came before country, however, these new brick
and mortar representations of the power and

00:03:42.739 --> 00:03:48.020
benefit of the federal government helped tie
the nation together, but the post office’s

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:51.280
impact didn’t stop at its mere existence.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:56.799
Newspapers shipped free—or at least close
to free—and that revolutionized the spread

00:03:56.799 --> 00:03:58.000
of information.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:03.560
In the 1850s, this meant that someone in brand
new Houston, Texas could read a newspaper

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:06.939
just as easily as someone in Washington, DC.

00:04:06.939 --> 00:04:11.299
If it had been more difficult to distribute
national newspapers, individuals wouldn’t

00:04:11.299 --> 00:04:14.280
have had information on national politics.

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:19.090
Without knowledge of national politics, the
state might have stayed as the dominant entity.

00:04:19.090 --> 00:04:23.610
The long overland routes to tiny frontier
towns absolutely did not make money, but the

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:26.300
post office wasn’t supposed to make money
at the time.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:31.740
It was there to make living in a frontier
town of 1,000 as similar as possible to living

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:34.539
in an east coast town of 100,000.

00:04:34.539 --> 00:04:40.530
150 years ago, it took more than a month and
a half to travel to many parts of the frontier

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:45.280
from New York… if you could get to them
at all, however, the post office needed faster

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:49.800
routes so it created post roads—long rudimentary
overland routes.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:55.030
With time and traffic, however, these post
roads developed into America’s first interstate

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:56.030
roads.

00:04:56.030 --> 00:04:58.979
The informational links led to a physical
links.

00:04:58.979 --> 00:05:05.330
A big question, however, was how to efficiently
get the mail out into and across the frontier.

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:11.340
In 1860, the railroad ended in western Missouri
and after that the mail had to go out over

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:12.340
land.

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:16.280
The Pony Express was a short lived commercial
express mail service connecting the end of

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:18.000
the railroad to California.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.970
In only 10 days, it could get a letter from
the Atlantic to the Pacific.

00:05:21.970 --> 00:05:24.139
This was unbelievable at the time.

00:05:24.139 --> 00:05:28.440
Once mail arrived at the end of the railroad
line in St Joseph’s, Missouri, a rider would

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:33.509
take the pouch of a mail and immediately start
riding west at up to 25 miles per hour—full

00:05:33.509 --> 00:05:38.100
speed—and then every 10 or so miles they’d
stop at a Pony express station to switch to

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:39.340
a fresh horse.

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:43.819
Every 75 miles, they’d switch riders to
keep the mail pouch thundering across the

00:05:43.819 --> 00:05:45.840
country day and night.

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:52.050
This didn’t come cheap—it was the equivalent
of $130 to send just one half ounce letter—but

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:53.900
time is money and people did pay.

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:58.849
One of the most famous rides of the shortlived
Pony Express was following the 1860 presidential

00:05:58.849 --> 00:05:59.849
election.

00:05:59.849 --> 00:06:03.539
Telegraph did exist at the time but it didn’t
stretch all the way across the country.

00:06:03.539 --> 00:06:07.879
The eastern line ended in Fort Kearny, Nebraska,
so immediately following the news of Lincoln’s

00:06:07.879 --> 00:06:10.139
win, a rider set out west.

00:06:10.139 --> 00:06:14.410
Using the relay system, riders made their
way across the country to Fort Churchill,

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:18.080
Nevada—the beginning of the western telegraph
line—to transmit the news.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:22.650
The west coast papers therefore published
the news of Lincoln’s win only seven days

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:25.830
and 17 hours behind the east coast papers.

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:30.600
Just two years earlier, it took 17 days for
news to cross the continent.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:33.389
This was an unbelievable feat.

00:06:33.389 --> 00:06:39.009
With the completion of the transcontinental
telegraph line in 1861, however, the Pony

00:06:39.009 --> 00:06:44.400
Express closed its doors after only 19 months
of operation, but it did play a big role in

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:46.290
the innovation of mail systems.

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:51.009
As mail volume rose to the west coast, the
US Post Office looked for ways to further

00:06:51.009 --> 00:06:53.150
shorten the travel time across the continent.

00:06:53.150 --> 00:06:58.830
Until the opening of the transcontinental
railroad in 1863, most transcontinental mail

00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:04.449
went out to Missouri by rail before switching
to lower cost stagecoach rather than the Pony

00:07:04.449 --> 00:07:05.449
Express.

00:07:05.449 --> 00:07:10.729
At first all westward mail was put on the
train to be sorted in Missouri, but in 1862

00:07:10.729 --> 00:07:14.439
the first post office on wheels was put into
operation.

00:07:14.439 --> 00:07:18.610
A staffer would sort the mail while on the
move in an identical way as a post office

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:23.460
on land so when it arrived in St Joseph’s,
Missouri, it was all split up into different

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:25.819
bundles for different destinations.

00:07:25.819 --> 00:07:30.740
This system proved incredibly successful and
in the coming decades, the post office gave

00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:36.110
exorbitant subsidies and payments to railroads
across the country to move mail.

00:07:36.110 --> 00:07:40.801
Most lines to small communities would never
have been financially viable without the post

00:07:40.801 --> 00:07:42.740
office contracts they attracted.

00:07:42.740 --> 00:07:48.139
The Post Office, therefore, was instrumental
in physically connecting small towns across

00:07:48.139 --> 00:07:50.680
America to the outside world.

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:52.449
Expansion was getting easy.

00:07:52.449 --> 00:07:57.050
This role in innovation happened once again
in in the 1920’s.

00:07:57.050 --> 00:08:01.340
Aviation became big during World War One,
but once peace was settled it almost became

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:02.710
irrelevant.

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:05.980
Civil aviation was at first nearly nonexistent.

00:08:05.980 --> 00:08:10.900
Just months after the end of World War One,
however, the first regularly scheduled airmail

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:13.220
route between DC and New York began.

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:19.509
By 1925, airmail routes stretched to hundreds
of towns all across the United States.

00:08:19.509 --> 00:08:23.960
Just like with the railroad, the post’s
subsidies allowed for air service to towns

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:27.919
that would never have been profitable with
passenger only flights.

00:08:27.919 --> 00:08:32.440
The US air mail service grew to become the
largest in the world making the US one of

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:37.540
the most connected countries in the world
and its legacy still survives today.

00:08:37.540 --> 00:08:42.250
Many of the airlines that merged into United,
Delta, and American Airlines were founded

00:08:42.250 --> 00:08:43.670
as airmail operators.

00:08:43.670 --> 00:08:50.020
It’s easy to forget that 200 years ago only
8 million people lived in the 20 states and

00:08:50.020 --> 00:08:52.570
four territories that formed the Union.

00:08:52.570 --> 00:08:57.530
The continent filled in incredibly fast and
a lot of credit has to be given to the postal

00:08:57.530 --> 00:08:58.530
system.

00:08:58.530 --> 00:09:02.850
Few countries as large as the US have such
a strong national identity.

00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:07.500
The spread of information is one of the greatest
determinants of a country’s success and

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:13.140
its unlikely the US would be such a global
superpower today without the education, connectivity,

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:16.150
and innovation that the post office bought.

00:09:16.150 --> 00:09:21.480
Of the five largest countries in the world,
the US is the only to not have a serious present-day

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:23.450
sovereignty movement within its borders.

00:09:23.450 --> 00:09:29.310
The has to be at least partially attributed
to how easy it has always been to spread information

00:09:29.310 --> 00:09:30.310
across the country.

00:09:30.310 --> 00:09:35.640
It may be obsolete today, but the post office
was once the peak of innovation—so much

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:42.580
so that it made America.

